Title: "Induction and Recursion"
Id: "0084ea96-4893-4c13-82b6-9c0c9c0ae648"


Text: "Induction and recursion represent the dual foundational aspects of the
       successor structure on $\mathbb{N}$.
       - *Mathematical induction* is the supreme method of proof: to verify that
         a proposition $P(n)$ holds for all natural numbers, it suffices to
         verify its truth at $0$ and show that truth is inherited from $n$ to
         $S(n)$.
       - *Primitive recursion* is the supreme method of construction: to define
         a sequence or function on $\mathbb{N}$, it suffices to specify an
         initial value and a deterministic rule for stepping forward.

       Both principles are logical manifestations of the same core fact:
       $\mathbb{N}$ contains no spurious elements beyond those generated from
       zero by repeated iteration of the successor function."
Id: "fe9c481f-c3a7-4367-8dde-a342561a4ccc"


SectionTitle: "Inductive Proofs"
Id: "29a987cf-22cb-4dc0-ad8c-4281456e5f57"


Theorem:
given: P(n_) is (_ is \natural) -> (_ is \\statement)
where:
. P(\natural.zero)
. forAll: n is \natural
  where: P(n)
  then: P(\natural.successor:of{n})
then:
. forAll: n is \natural
  then: P(n)
Documented:
. called: "Principle of Mathematical Induction"
Proof: """Let $A = \{n \in \mathbb{N} : P(n)\}$. By hypothesis, $P(0)$ holds, so
          $0 \in A$. Furthermore, for any $n \in A$, $P(n)$ holds, which implies
          $P(S(n))$, so $S(n) \in A$. Thus $A$ contains $0$ and is closed under
          successor. By the induction postulate of the Peano axioms,
          $A = \mathbb{N}$, which proves that $P(n)$ holds for all
          $n \in \mathbb{N}$."""
Id: "3940cf8b-62b7-41cf-984a-62429aeb835e"


Theorem:
given: P(n_) is (_ is \natural) -> (_ is \\statement)
where:
. forAll: n is \natural
  then:
  . if:
    . forAll: k is \natural
      where:
      . k \:natural.leq?:/ n
      . k != n
      then: P(k)
    then: P(n)
then:
. forAll: n is \natural
  then: P(n)
Documented:
. called: "Principle of Strong Induction"
. description: "If $P(n)$ follows from the validity of $P(k)$ at every preceding
                natural number $k<n$, then $P$ holds for every natural number."
Proof: """Define the auxiliary predicate $Q(n)$ by
          $$Q(n) \iff \forall k < n, \, P(k)$$ We prove
          $\forall n \in \mathbb{N}, Q(n)$ by ordinary mathematical induction on
          $n$:
          1. *Base Case ($n = 0$)*: The statement $Q(0)$ asserts that $P(k)$
             holds for all $k < 0$. Because no natural number is strictly less
             than $0$, this condition is vacuously true.
          2. *Inductive Step*: Assume $Q(n)$ holds, meaning $P(k)$ is true for
             all $k < n$. By the strong induction hypothesis, this implies
             $P(n)$. Combining $Q(n)$ (truth for all $k < n$) with $P(n)$ (truth
             at $n$) proves that $P(k)$ holds for all $k < n + 1$, which is
             precisely $Q(n + 1)$. By ordinary induction, $Q(n)$ holds for all
             $n \in \mathbb{N}$. Since $Q(n + 1)$ implies $P(n)$, it follows
             that $P(n)$ holds for every natural number $n$."""
Id: "844c54eb-187b-4369-8fd2-84d5d7da2b4b"


Text: "A rigorous inductive proof demands careful epistemic hygiene: the
       *induction hypothesis* $P(n)$ is assumed strictly for a fixed, arbitrary
       index $n$, and must be used solely to derive the subsequent assertion
       $P(n + 1)$. In strong induction (complete induction), all preceding
       instances $P(0), P(1), \dots, P(n-1)$ are available as simultaneous
       hypotheses, which is particularly powerful when dealing with recurrences
       like the Fibonacci sequence or the prime factorization of integers."
Id: "a73f0f65-b93a-4380-8623-255523e3a354"


Theorem:
given: A \:(nonempty)::subset:/ \naturals
then:
. exists: m is \natural
  suchThat:
  . m "in"? A
  . forAll: n is \natural
    where: n "in"? A
    then: m \:natural.leq?:/ n
Documented:
. called: "Well-Ordering Principle for the Natural Numbers"
. description: "Every nonempty subset of $\mathbb{N}$ has a least element."
Proof: """Suppose for contradiction that $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is nonempty
          yet possesses no least element. Let $P(n)$ be the assertion that
          $n \notin A$. We prove that $P(n)$ holds for all $n \in \mathbb{N}$ by
          strong induction. Let $n \in \mathbb{N}$, and assume that $P(k)$ holds
          for all $k < n$, meaning no natural number strictly smaller than $n$
          belongs to $A$. If $n$ were in $A$, then because no strictly smaller
          natural number belongs to $A$, $n$ would be the least element of $A$,
          contradicting our hypothesis that $A$ has no least element. Therefore
          $n \notin A$, which establishes $P(n)$. By strong induction,
          $n \notin A$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, which forces
          $A = \emptyset$. This contradicts the assumption that $A$ is nonempty."""
Id: "dd21ebd6-0821-429e-af26-123c01ca0432"


SectionTitle: "Recursive Definitions"
Id: "fc540913-8908-49b4-b6bf-253901c18952"


TextTheorem: "Recursion theorem on $\mathbb{N}$. Let $X$ be a set, choose
              $x_0\in X$, and let $F:\mathbb{N}\times X\to X$. There exists a
              unique function $a:\mathbb{N}\to X$ satisfying $a(0)=x_0$ and
              $a(n+1)=F(n,a(n))$ for every $n$. The theorem provides both
              existence and uniqueness; recursive equations alone should not be
              treated as a definition until this result is available."
Id: "15ab06d1-8e34-450b-8e17-e985c387bd96"


[\sequence:in{X}]
Declares: a(n__) is \function:on{\naturals}:to{X}
when: X is \set
Documented:
. called: "sequence in $X?$"
. written: "(a?_{n})_{n\in\mathbb{N}}"
. description: "A sequence in $X$ is a function from $\mathbb{N}$ to $X$; its
                indices record positions, so equal values may occur at different
                indices."
Id: "d9daf505-ce5f-45be-baae-06c06602fa79"


Text: "Addition and multiplication are canonical applications of recursion.
       Fixing $m$, addition is the unique function satisfying $0+m=m$ and
       $(n+1)+m=(n+m)+1$; multiplication is the unique function satisfying
       $0\cdot m=0$ and $(n+1)\cdot m=n\cdot m+m$. Algebraic laws such as
       associativity and distributivity are theorems proved by induction, not
       clauses silently included in these recursive definitions."
Id: "63deb9e9-5cbd-4507-b7b3-f0047aa4b712"


SectionTitle: "Minimality and Universal Character"
Id: "ede13b47-dfdb-4898-bc0e-3043dc9712dd"


TextTheorem: "Any two Peano systems are uniquely isomorphic by a map preserving
              zero and successor. More precisely, if $(N,0,S)$ and $(N',0',S')$
              satisfy the Peano axioms, there is a unique bijection $f:N\to N'$
              such that $f(0)=0'$ and $f(S(n))=S'(f(n))$. Thus the natural
              numbers are determined up to unique structure-preserving
              isomorphism."
Id: "3d9d5fae-a692-4d51-8b38-1f39fde29d68"


Text: "This categoricity is the reason one may move between abstract natural
       numbers and von Neumann ordinals without changing arithmetic. The
       underlying sets are different descriptions; the successor structures are
       isomorphic. Properties expressible solely in terms of zero, successor,
       and definitions derived from them are transported along the unique
       isomorphism."
Id: "2866d2b7-05e9-483a-8339-08e9feb3fb8c"
